<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asiento</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The asiento was a contract granted by the Spanish crown giving a person or company the right to supply enslaved Africans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. In 1713 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> granted the asiento to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which passed it to the South Sea Company. It strengthened Britain's role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> during the formative period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asiento.jpg" alt="Asiento" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1713 Treaty between Britain and Spain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1713_Asiento_contract.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Spanish an asiento was a contract, and in public law any contract made for public utility between the government and private individuals. The 1479 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, the principal source of slave labour, to Portugal, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> contracted the trade out to foreign merchants. As the indigenous population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> collapsed and critics such as Bartolomé de Las Casas opposed the exploitation of indigenous labour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a> allowed enslaved people to be brought directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The first asiento, of August 1518, gave his Flemish favourite Laurent de Gouvenot a monopoly of up to 4,000 for eight years, which he sold to Genoese merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> for 25,000 ducats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Enslaved Africans were treated as merchandise: the crown collected a duty on each pieza, not on each person delivered, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> controlled the trade. Merchant banks bid for the contract and worked with shipping traders. In the early 17th century the main ports of arrival were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, from which captives were sent on to Venezuela, the Antilles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, and many brought beyond the licensed quota were sold illegally. Between 1595 and 1622 about half of all those imported went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Iberian Union Portuguese financiers, with Angolan interests dominant, held the large asiento established in 1595. After Portugal's independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> refused it to the Portuguese and turned to the Genoese bankers Grillo and Lomellini, who in 1662 promised 24,000 captives in seven years, and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> through Curaçao. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a>, the French Guinea Company held it for the import of 48,000 people over a decade, with the kings of Spain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> each holding a quarter of its capital.</p><p>Under the Peace of Utrecht Britain's contract allowed 4,800 a year for 30 years, offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and other ports, a yearly ship of 500 tons of general goods, and a quarter of the profits for the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It ended with the 1750 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> after the war the Spanish called the Guerra del Asiento. A group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> held it from 1765 to 1779.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asiento de Negros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Utrecht (1713) brought the first large imperial gains: Gibraltar, Minorca, Nova Scotia, Newfoundland, the Hudson Bay territories and the <b>asiento</b> (the contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America).</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, won at Ramillies in 1706, Oudenarde in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia and the <b>asiento</b> to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 16th century some 200,000 enslaved Africans reached New Spain and some 95,000 Peru, by modern estimates, many under the <b>asiento</b> contracts the crown sold to foreign traders.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
